WEBVTT

NOTE Automatically generated timing; text edited for apparent transcription errors. Errors may remain.

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:04.938
Hello, my name is Matthew Baerg, and
this is my CS 499 code review of

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:08.570
MultiTasked, my Kotlin Android task-
management application.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:12.638
This is a re-recorded walkthrough of the
preserved original application.

00:00:12.638 --> 00:00:15.988
I'll explain its existing structure,
identify specific strengths and

00:00:15.988 --> 00:00:16.530
weaknesses,

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.778
and describe the enhancement plan across
software design and engineering,

00:00:20.778 --> 00:00:23.190
algorithms and data structures, and
databases.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:26.430
The enhancements are also available
separately in my portfolio,

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.712
but the main review will stay focused on
the original code and the reasons

00:00:29.712 --> 00:00:30.510
for those changes.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.543
MultiTasked organizes tasks into shared
boards. The source includes

00:00:34.543 --> 00:00:37.230
registration and sign-in, creating and
joining boards,

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:41.826
adding editing tasks, completing tasks,
assigning them to board members,

00:00:41.826 --> 00:00:42.770
deleting records.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:47.710
Grocery boards also use task prices and
running totals.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:52.315
I selected this artifact because it
connects several areas of software

00:00:52.315 --> 00:00:53.770
development in one application.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.213
A change to the interface can affect
state management, task ordering,

00:00:57.213 --> 00:00:58.810
persistence, and error handling.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:03.009
That makes it useful for demonstrating
how I evaluate an existing design and

00:01:03.009 --> 00:01:04.210
implement plan improvements.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:08.255
I will distinguish behavior supported by
the source from behavior that still

00:01:08.255 --> 00:01:09.670
needs integration and testing.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.611
Reading the code alone does not prove
that every Firebase backend workflow

00:01:13.611 --> 00:01:14.030
succeeds.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.330
The task model shows the information the
application already supports.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:21.630
Each task has an identifier, a board
identifier, and a title.

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:25.549
It also stores completion status, a
creation timestamp, notes, and an

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:28.470
optional due date, a priority, and an
optional assignee.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:34.501
These fields matter because the
algorithm's enhancement does not need to

00:01:34.501 --> 00:01:36.810
invent an entirely new task model.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:41.293
Creation time can support recent first
ordering, while due date and priority

00:01:41.293 --> 00:01:43.450
can support deadline and urgency
ordering.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:48.403
One detail to notice is that the
completion status and creation time are

00:01:48.403 --> 00:01:49.710
mutable properties.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.370
A data structure that depends on those
values must account for changes.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:58.093
For example, if a priority queue stores
a task and that task's ordering

00:01:58.093 --> 00:01:59.730
fields change unexpectedly,

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:02.530
the queue can stop representing the
intended order.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:07.282
That is one reason to use controlled
snapshots when performing enhanced

00:02:07.282 --> 00:02:08.050
sorting work.

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.930
The original application already has a
useful repository boundary.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.998
This interface describes board and task
operations without putting Firestore

00:02:16.998 --> 00:02:18.550
calls directly in the screen.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:24.470
Reads expose flows of data and writes
using suspending functions.

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:29.311
The broader application already uses
Compose, ViewModel, StateFlow, and

00:02:29.311 --> 00:02:29.610
Hilt.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:33.992
I am therefore not claiming that the
enhancement introduces MVVM from

00:02:33.992 --> 00:02:34.430
scratch.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:38.384
My goal is to make the existing
separation more consistent and easier to

00:02:38.384 --> 00:02:38.610
test.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:42.860
This file is the main software design
review target at the beginning of the

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.050
board details screen.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:47.510
The composable collects states from the
view model.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:52.282
Further down, the same file handles
screen structure, user controls, task

00:02:52.282 --> 00:02:55.170
list rendering, task rows, and editing
behavior.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.459
This code provides useful functionality,
but it concentrates several

00:03:03.459 --> 00:03:04.840
responsibilities in one place

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.279
and makes it harder to inspect a visual
component without also understanding

00:03:08.279 --> 00:03:11.040
the view model connection and the
surrounding state behavior.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.821
Changes to a task editor also require
working inside a much larger screen

00:03:14.821 --> 00:03:15.080
file.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.780
Another concrete issue is the
celebration effect.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.872
There are two celebration call sites
connected to the same celebration

00:03:20.872 --> 00:03:21.120
state.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:25.754
This duplication makes the intended
feedback harder to reason about and

00:03:25.754 --> 00:03:28.020
creates potential duplicate rendering.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.000
The enhancement plan was to separate a
route from rendering screen.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:39.057
The route would own the lifecycle-aware
state collection and the connection

00:03:39.057 --> 00:03:40.280
to the view model.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:43.605
The screen will receive state and
callbacks, which makes its

00:03:43.605 --> 00:03:44.880
responsibilities clear.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:51.136
I also plan to plan an explicit action
contract for events such as adding,

00:03:51.136 --> 00:03:53.480
completing, or deleting a task.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:58.435
Smaller composables would handle focused
parts of the interface and the task

00:03:58.435 --> 00:04:01.180
row and editor would move into their own
file.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.180
This introduces more small components
and files, so it is a trade-off.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:08.889
The benefit is that each part has a
clear purpose, making changes and

00:04:08.889 --> 00:04:10.340
testing easier to organize.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.674
The addTask and updatePrice methods also
need explicit validation at this

00:04:16.674 --> 00:04:17.280
boundary.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.740
A disabled button is not enough to
guarantee valid input.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:25.611
The view model should reject blank task
titles and prices that are negative

00:04:25.611 --> 00:04:28.140
or not finite before calling the
repository.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:33.370
In toggleTaskDone, the celebration state
can be set before the repository

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:34.540
confirms the update.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:38.600
If the write fails, the interface may
have already indicated success.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.560
Enhancement moves that feedback after
the successful write.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:46.530
The shared error handling helper also
needs to preserve coroutine

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:47.220
cancellation.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:52.138
Cancellation is part of a normal life
cycle behavior, so it should be

00:04:52.138 --> 00:04:55.620
rethrown rather than turned into an
ordinary failure message.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:00.623
The user should receive a stable message
and logging should be best effort,

00:05:00.623 --> 00:05:04.000
so a logging failure does not hide the
original problem.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.846
Finally, this view model depends on the
concrete SettingsRepository, even

00:05:07.846 --> 00:05:10.020
though it needs only the celebration
preference.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.192
A narrow CelebrationSettings interface
makes the dependencies smaller and

00:05:14.192 --> 00:05:15.800
allow a simple fake in unit tests.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:19.940
These changes demonstrate separation of
responsibility, validation,

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:22.680
dependency design, and safer
asynchronous behavior.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.260
They improve this feature's error path.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.802
They do not establish that every logging
or security concern elsewhere in

00:05:28.802 --> 00:05:30.340
the application has been resolved.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:33.973
The original sorting options include
recent, alphabetical, ascending and

00:05:33.973 --> 00:05:36.900
descending, completed first, or
incomplete first ordering.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.180
These use standard Kotlin collection
operations.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:41.620
The recent option has a correctness
problem.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:46.980
It sorts tasks by ID. An ID identifies a
document.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:50.100
It does not express the creation time
rule that the label promises.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:54.372
The task model already has createdAt, so
the software-design enhancement

00:05:54.372 --> 00:05:57.340
first extracts sorting policy, and
corrects that key.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:02.265
The larger algorithm's enhancement
addresses a separate need, ordering
tasks

00:06:02.265 --> 00:06:03.820
by deadline and urgency.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:09.264
The original application stores due
dates and priorities, but the sorting

00:06:09.264 --> 00:06:10.580
options are missing.

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:15.640
For due date ordering, the plan uses a
custom, stable merge sort.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:20.019
Dated tasks come before tasks without
dates, earlier dates come first, and

00:06:20.019 --> 00:06:21.740
priority resolves with equal dates.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:27.105
When both keys are equal, the algorithm
preserves the input order, choosing

00:06:27.105 --> 00:06:31.707
the left item when two values compare
equally, is what preserves stability

00:06:31.707 --> 00:06:32.640
during a merge.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:37.600
Merge sort takes O of n log n time, and
O of n auxiliary storage.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:40.160
Plus, its recursion stack.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:44.660
It gives predictable sorting behavior,
but the extra storage is a cost.

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:49.680
For urgency ordering, the plan uses an
array-backed binary min-heap.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:54.526
Incomplete tasks come first, though, and
overdue status, priority, due date,

00:06:54.526 --> 00:06:55.720
creation time, and ID.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:58.400
Completed tasks are never treated as
overdue.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:03.139
These rules need to be explicit because
the word urgency by itself is too

00:07:03.139 --> 00:07:03.940
vague to test.

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:09.005
A heap can inspect the next item in
constant time and remove it in

00:07:09.005 --> 00:07:10.160
logarithmic time.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:18.065
However, building and draining the queue
for a complete task list still

00:07:18.065 --> 00:07:20.180
takes O of n log n.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:25.643
I'm not claiming that it is
asymptotically faster than merge sort,
or that

00:07:25.643 --> 00:07:29.420
it produces a measured improvement in
Android frame rate.

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:32.400
The original persistence path uses
Firebase.

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:36.852
Boards are stored in the boards
collection, and each board has a task

00:07:36.852 --> 00:07:37.540
subcollection.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:42.285
The read methods observe snapshots and
map documents into the application

00:07:42.285 --> 00:07:42.740
models.

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:46.380
The code also implements create, update,
and delete operations.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:50.779
There is a real persistence layer, so
the database enhancement should not

00:07:50.779 --> 00:07:54.040
describe the starting point as an
application with no database.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:57.320
The missing piece is an implemented
local Room workspace.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:01.760
There is consistency risk to review in
the original cloud code,

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:06.249
In addTask, writing the task and
incrementing the boards task count are

00:08:06.249 --> 00:08:08.020
separate awaited operations.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:12.392
If the first succeeds and the second
fails, the stored count can disagree

00:08:12.392 --> 00:08:13.500
with the actual task.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.420
Deletion also changes the task and count
separately.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:21.377
The board deletion method retrieves the
task delete, retrieves the task,

00:08:21.377 --> 00:08:24.460
deletes them one at a time, and then
deletes the board.

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:28.835
That sequence can be interrupted and the
number of remote operations grow

00:08:28.835 --> 00:08:29.880
with the task count.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:34.575
These are reasons to review atomicity,
recovery, and scale rather than

00:08:34.575 --> 00:08:39.269
assuming that separated successful calls
always produce one consistent

00:08:39.269 --> 00:08:39.940
operation.

00:08:41.060 --> 00:08:44.080
These observations inform the local
database design.

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:48.932
They are not a claim that adding Room
repairs the existing Firebase

00:08:48.932 --> 00:08:49.620
operations.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:53.220
The local and shared storage paths
require their own validation.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:57.683
The original local database folder
contains an empty placeholder for the

00:08:57.683 --> 00:09:00.100
database, entities, DAOs, and mappings.

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:04.100
A folder named local is not evidence
that local persistence works.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:08.280
These files provide a concrete starting
point for the database enhancement.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:14.472
Enhancement plan was to implement Room
entities for boards and tasks, data

00:09:14.472 --> 00:09:15.520
access objects,

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:20.009
and for create, read, update, and delete
operations in a repository that

00:09:20.009 --> 00:09:22.020
connects persistence to the interface.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:27.300
Each task should reference a valid board
through a required foreign key.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:31.460
Deleting a board should actually cascade
to its task.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:36.104
Creating a board with an optional first
task should use a transaction so

00:09:36.104 --> 00:09:38.940
either both inserts succeed or neither
remains.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:44.424
Task counts should be calculated from
the stored task row rather than

00:09:44.424 --> 00:09:46.300
maintained as a second value.

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:50.447
An index beginning with board ID
supports queries within a board with a

00:09:50.447 --> 00:09:52.900
trade-off of extra storage and write
work.

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:58.720
Tasks and deletions should check both
the board and task identifiers.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:04.175
Bound query parameters keep supplied
value separate from SQL syntax while

00:10:04.175 --> 00:10:08.320
repository validation rejects invalid
titles and oversized input.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:12.703
The scope is explicit on this device
workspace alongside with existing

00:10:12.703 --> 00:10:14.040
shared board's workspace.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:18.663
Room does not replace all Firebase
functionality and does not synchronize

00:10:18.663 --> 00:10:20.500
local records with Firestore.

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:25.023
Making that separation visible avoids
promising synchronization that has not

00:10:25.023 --> 00:10:25.900
been implemented.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:31.605
The migration example evolves a
documented local version 1 test schema
to

00:10:31.605 --> 00:10:34.140
version 2 while retaining existing rows.

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:38.485
Because the original application has no
active Room implementation, this is

00:10:38.485 --> 00:10:41.840
not a claim that the shipped database or
Firebase record were migrated.

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:46.326
Security requires reviewing the actual
access rules as well as the

00:10:46.326 --> 00:10:46.900
interface.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.907
These supplied Firestore rules check
whether the request is authenticated

00:10:50.907 --> 00:10:53.520
and whether the user is an owner or
member of the board.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:57.495
That shows an intended access boundary
that is needed for their validation,

00:10:57.495 --> 00:10:59.020
but it needs further validation.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:03.365
The helper reads an existing board
document so new board creation and

00:11:03.365 --> 00:11:05.460
joining behavior needs careful review.

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:11.373
This file also does not define access
rules for users and errors collections

00:11:11.373 --> 00:11:13.240
used by the application.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:19.094
I have not verified that these are the
rules deployed to the live Firebase

00:11:19.094 --> 00:11:19.660
project.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:25.476
For local storage, parameterized
queries, validation, foreign keys, and

00:11:25.476 --> 00:11:28.060
backup exclusions address specific
risks.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.660
They do not provide complete protection.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:35.042
Room does not automatically encrypt the
database and the local workspace is

00:11:35.042 --> 00:11:38.080
available to anyone using the same app
installation.

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:41.120
It is not separated by Firebase account.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.220
Signing out of Firebase does not clear
it.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:47.900
My security goal is to identify these
boundaries and limitations accurately.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:52.080
Sensitive use cases would require
additional controls and testing.

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:57.580
Publishing the portfolio also requires
excluding private configuration.

00:11:57.580 --> 00:12:01.520
Signing material and personal data from
downloadable code.

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:07.580
The original local unit test checks that
2 plus 2 equals 4.

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:12.060
It confirms a basic test setup but does
not test task-management behavior.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:16.086
Enhancement plan therefore includes
tests tied to actual requirements like

00:12:16.086 --> 00:12:19.595
input validation, state summaries,
ordering rules, failure behavior, and

00:12:19.595 --> 00:12:20.180
persistence.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:24.700
The program outcomes connect to specific
evidence.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:28.402
The architecture and database
implementation support effective use of

00:12:28.402 --> 00:12:29.260
computing tools.

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:33.385
The sorting rules, custom algorithms,
and complexity analysis support

00:12:33.385 --> 00:12:35.760
algorithmic reasoning and design trade-
offs.

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:41.268
This review, the narratives, and the
source explanations support

00:12:41.268 --> 00:12:45.660
professional communication, validation,
sorry, communication.

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:50.409
The validation access rules review and
explicit privacy limitations support

00:12:50.409 --> 00:12:51.480
a security mindset.

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:56.193
For collaboration and decision-making,
my evidence includes instructor

00:12:56.193 --> 00:12:58.560
feedback and the changes made in
responses.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:03.560
For example, my instructor asked for a
clear presentation of my own work and

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:06.660
the video recording’s audio and upload
access to be corrected.

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:09.059
[Closing sentence about a repository and
professional team collaboration is

00:13:09.059 --> 00:13:10.200
unclear in the automated transcription.]
